  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 2,
    "lesson_title": "Signals Whose Fourier Transform Does Not Converge",
    "lesson_core_move": "Determine whether a continuous-time signal lacks a convergent Fourier transform by testing the Fourier-transform integral for convergence and interpreting nondecaying or growing behavior as requiring Laplace-domain exponential weighting.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
        "lesson_title": "Signals Whose Fourier Transform Does Not Converge",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Signals Whose Fourier Transform Does Not Converge",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "X(j\\omega)=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}x(t)e^{-j\\omega t}\\,dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "To use Fourier analysis, start by writing the transform definition exactly as $X(j\\omega)=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}x(t)e^{-j\\omega t}dt$. The first object the learner must recognize is this integral template, and the first rule is that it is the decision point for every signal in this lesson.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Because $|e^{-j\\omega t}|=1$, convergence is controlled only by $x(t)$. The convergence test is an absolute-value check on the same support in the transform, written as $\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}|x(t)|\\,dt < \\infty$. This is the practical rule used on each unbounded interval of time support.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\int_0^{\\infty} e^{-at} \\,dt = \\frac{1}{a},\\quad a>0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use the base case $x(t)=e^{-at}u(t)$ with $a>0$ as the convergent anchor. It satisfies the magnitude test, so it has a valid Fourier-transform result and sets the minimum standard for this lesson.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Contrast that with signals that do not decay: constant or growing tails can make $\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}|x(t)|dt$ diverge, so the Fourier transform is not guaranteed in the ordinary $j\\omega$ sense. The move is to classify the signal first, before doing any additional manipulation.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "After this screen, keep only convergent signals for the ordinary Fourier-frequency viewpoint, and flag nondecaying or growing behavior as cases that trigger the upcoming Laplace-domain exponential weighting move.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
        "lesson_title": "Signals Whose Fourier Transform Does Not Converge",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Testing Fourier-Transform Integral Convergence",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Test convergence of $X(j\\omega)=\\int_{0}^{\\infty}e^{-2t}e^{-j\\omega t}dt$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The signal is one-sided because of $u(t)$, so write the Fourier integral as a one-sided improper integral: $X(j\\omega)=\\int_{0}^{\\infty}e^{-2t}e^{-j\\omega t}\\,dt$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use the cue that the exponential kernel has unit magnitude, so $\\left|e^{-2t}e^{-j\\omega t}\\right|=e^{-2t}$. Test convergence on this envelope instead of the full complex signal.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\int_0^\\infty |e^{-2t}e^{-j\\omega t}|\\,dt=\\int_0^\\infty e^{-2t}\\,dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\int_0^\\infty e^{-2t}\\,dt=\\left[-\\frac{1}{2}e^{-2t}\\right]_0^\\infty=\\frac{1}{2}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Since the absolute-value integral is finite, the Fourier-transform integral is convergent in the standard sense. Therefore this decaying one-sided signal is accepted by the $j\\omega$-axis rule.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
        "lesson_title": "Signals Whose Fourier Transform Does Not Converge",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Recognizing Nondecaying Signals That Do Not Converge",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Determine convergence for $x(t)=\\cos(\\omega_0 t)$ over $(-\\infty,\\infty)$ using magnitude behavior.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Write the ordinary Fourier-transform integral over the full time axis: $X(j\\omega)=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}\\cos(\\omega_0 t)e^{-j\\omega t}\\,dt$. The support is unbounded, so convergence depends on what happens along the tails.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The Fourier kernel has unit magnitude, so it cannot make a nondecaying signal shrink: $|e^{-j\\omega t}|=1$. The cosine factor remains bounded but does not decay toward zero.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "|\\cos(\\omega_0 t)e^{-j\\omega t}|=|\\cos(\\omega_0 t)|",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}|\\cos(\\omega_0 t)|\\,dt=\\infty",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Because the magnitude integral has infinite area on the unbounded interval, the Fourier transform does not converge in the ordinary improper-integral sense. Boundedness alone is not enough; the signal must decay enough for the integral test to pass.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This is the diagnostic pattern for nondecaying periodic signals: they may be finite in amplitude, but their continuing tails keep the Fourier integral from converging. Growing signals are the next extreme and also fail this standard Fourier test.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
        "lesson_title": "Signals Whose Fourier Transform Does Not Converge",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Recognizing Growing Signals That Do Not Converge",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Test convergence of $x(t)=e^{3t}u(t)$ by evaluating the implied weighted-free magnitude integral.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Start with the ordinary Fourier-transform setup. Because $u(t)$ restricts the signal to $t\\ge 0$, the integral only has a right-sided tail to check.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "X(j\\omega)=\\int_{0}^{\\infty}e^{3t}e^{-j\\omega t}\\,dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The Fourier kernel does not damp the signal because $|e^{-j\\omega t}|=1$. So the magnitude behavior is controlled by the growing factor $e^{3t}$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\left|e^{3t}e^{-j\\omega t}\\right|=e^{3t}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now test the corresponding magnitude integral on $[0,\\infty)$. Its antiderivative grows without bound as the upper limit increases.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\int_{0}^{\\infty}e^{3t}\\,dt=\\lim_{T\\to\\infty}\\int_{0}^{T}e^{3t}\\,dt=\\lim_{T\\to\\infty}\\frac{e^{3T}-1}{3}=\\infty",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Therefore $x(t)=e^{3t}u(t)$ does not have a convergent ordinary Fourier transform. This is a growth-induced failure: the signal needs exponential weighting before a transform integral can converge.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 2,
        "lesson_title": "Signals Whose Fourier Transform Does Not Converge",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Identifying Signals That Need Exponential Weighting",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Find the condition on $\\sigma$ for $x(t)=e^{2t}u(t)$ so that $\\int_0^{\\infty}e^{(2-\\sigma)t}dt$ converges.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Cue: the signal $x(t)=e^{2t}u(t)$ grows on the right, so the previous unweighted Fourier test fails. Introduce exponential weighting and test the modified integral with parameter $\\sigma$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Choose the weighted Fourier-style form that isolates the real exponential factor: for a causal support, write",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "X_{\\sigma}(j\\omega)=\\int_0^{\\infty} e^{-\\sigma t}\\,e^{2t}u(t)e^{-j\\omega t}\\,dt = \\int_0^{\\infty} e^{(2-\\sigma)t}e^{-j\\omega t}\\,dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use magnitude logic next: $\\left|e^{-j\\omega t}\\right|=1$, so the oscillatory part does not affect convergence. Convergence depends on $\\int_0^{\\infty} e^{(2-\\sigma)t}\\,dt$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\int_0^{\\infty}\\left|e^{-\\sigma t}e^{2t}u(t)e^{-j\\omega t}\\right|\\,dt=\\int_0^{\\infty}e^{(2-\\sigma)t}\\,dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For an improper exponential tail, the one-sided integral is finite only when the exponent is negative: $2-\\sigma<0$. Convert this to a condition on $\\sigma$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "2-\\sigma<0 \\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\; \\sigma>2",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Result: $x(t)=e^{2t}u(t)$ needs a weight with $\\sigma>2$ for weighted-convergence. This is the first corrective template before generalizing to $e^{at}u(t)$ with condition $\\sigma>a$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
  "question_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 2,
